  id: "ch1_2",
  title: "Ch 1-2: Introduction to Environmental Concepts",
  short: "Ch 1-2 Intro",
  quiz: "Chapter 2 Quiz - Introduction to Environmental Concepts (19/21)",
  overview: "Introductory chapters. The exam guide says these are mostly ideas that get covered in more depth later. Focus on: ecology vs environmental science, the big issues facing the field, sustainability and the ecological footprint, the historical figures and philosophies (utilitarian conservation vs biocentric preservation), the events that launched modern environmentalism, resource inequity, and the Tragedy of the Commons.",
  guide: [
    { q: "Define and differentiate Ecology and Environmental Science. How are they similar and different?",
      a: "Ecology is the scientific study of how organisms interact with each other and with their physical environment. It is a pure natural science. Environmental Science is a systematic, interdisciplinary approach to learning about the environment and our place in it. It uses ecology but also physics, chemistry, geology, economics, politics, and ethics. Similar: both study the relationships between living things and their surroundings. Different: environmental science is mission oriented, its fundamental basis is human impact on the Earth, and it looks for solutions. Quiz: 'Environmental science is a systematic approach in learning about the environment' and 'the fundamental basis of environmental science is human impact on the Earth.'" },
    { q: "What are the major issues facing the field today?",
      a: "Rapid human population growth (about 9 billion projected for 2050), climate change, loss of biodiversity and habitat, pollution and toxic waste, depletion of fossil fuels and other nonrenewable resources, water scarcity, soil loss, hunger, and the inequitable distribution of wealth and resource use. Rapid population growth is the classic example of a cause of environmental problems; a more even distribution of wealth and sustainable resource use are not." },
    { q: "Explain ecological resources, sustainability, and the ecological footprint.",
      a: "Resources are renewable (replenished on a human time scale: forests, fish, fresh water, soil, solar energy) or nonrenewable (fixed stock: fossil fuels, minerals). Fossil fuel supplies are being steadily depleted. Sustainability means using resources at or below the rate they renew so future generations can meet their own needs. An ecological footprint is the area of biologically productive land and water needed to supply one person (or nation) with resources and absorb their wastes. If everyone lived like people in the United States our current resources would run out quickly (we would need several Earths). The way to shrink a footprint is to decrease consumption rates." },
    { q: "Identify the philosophy or significant contributions of Teddy Roosevelt, Gifford Pinchot, John Muir, Aldo Leopold, and Rachel Carson.",
      a: "Theodore Roosevelt (president 1901-1909): established the framework of the national forest, park, and wildlife refuge systems; pragmatic utilitarian conservation. Gifford Pinchot: first chief of the US Forest Service; utilitarian conservation, 'the greatest good for the greatest number for the longest time'; resources should be used wisely, not locked up. John Muir: founder of the Sierra Club; biocentric preservation; nature has inherent value and should be preserved for its own sake, led the fight against the Hetch Hetchy dam. Aldo Leopold: wildlife ecologist, 'A Sand County Almanac', the land ethic (humans are members of the biotic community, not conquerors); bridge between science and ethics. Rachel Carson: 'Silent Spring' (1962) exposed the dangers of DDT and pesticides and awakened the public to pollution and toxic chemicals, starting the modern environmentalism movement." },
    { q: "Compare and contrast utilitarian conservation with biocentric preservation.",
      a: "Utilitarian conservation (Pinchot, Roosevelt, the US Forest Service): nature is a resource for people; use it wisely and efficiently for the greatest good, for the greatest number, for the longest time; emphasizes instrumental value. Biocentric preservation (Muir, the National Park Service): other organisms and natural objects have inherent (intrinsic) value and a fundamental right to exist and pursue their own interests; nature should be preserved for its own sake, not only for its usefulness. Quiz pairing: National Park Service = biocentric preservationist; Forest Service = pragmatic utilitarian conservationist." },
    { q: "What major environmental events helped bring about the modern discipline?",
      a: "Hetch Hetchy Valley dam fight (1913) - Muir vs Pinchot, first big argument for the inherent value of trees, rocks, and wildlife. The Dust Bowl (1930s). Silent Spring (1962). The Cuyahoga River catching fire and the Santa Barbara oil spill (1969). First Earth Day and creation of the EPA (1970), followed by the Clean Air Act, Clean Water Act, and Endangered Species Act (1973). Love Canal toxic waste (1978). Later: Chernobyl, Exxon Valdez, the ozone hole, and climate change, which pushed the field into global environmentalism." },
    { q: "What is the message of the resource-inequity statistics? For example, what does the US spend more on: weapons of mass destruction or humanitarian aid?",
      a: "The message is that resource use and wealth are extremely uneven. About 4/5 of the world's population lives in countries where nearly everyone is poor by North American standards, and most of those countries are in Asia. The richest 20 percent of people consume most of the world's resources. The United States spends far more on its military and weapons than on humanitarian aid to the impoverished. 'Toxic colonialism' describes rich nations exporting toxic wastes to poor communities. Poverty and environmental degradation reinforce each other, so environmental problems are also social justice problems." },
    { q: "Describe the Principle of the Tragedy of the Commons. Can you provide an example?",
      a: "Garrett Hardin (1968). When a resource is shared and open to all (a commons), each user gets the full benefit of using more of it while the cost of overuse is spread across everyone. Acting rationally in their own interest, each user takes more, and the commons is destroyed. Examples: overgrazing a shared village pasture, overfishing the oceans, pumping a shared aquifer dry, dumping pollution into the atmosphere or a river, deforestation of public land. Solutions involve regulation, private ownership, quotas, or community agreements. This is a running theme in the lecture notes." },
    { q: "What does sustainability mean and what are the three pillars?",
      a: "Sustainable development: 'meeting the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs' (Brundtland Commission). Ideally it improves living conditions for generations in the future, and proponents argue development can proceed with minimal cost to the environment. The three pillars (the three E's): Environment (ecology), Economy, and Equity (society). Sustainable use of a resource requires understanding its rate of renewal and the ability to manage the rate of use." },
    { q: "Review of ethics terms: inherent vs instrumental value, ethics, moral agents.",
      a: "Ethics is the branch of philosophy concerned with right and wrong and the ultimate worth of actions or things. Instrumental value = worth because of usefulness to humans (utilitarian view). Inherent (intrinsic) value = worth in and of itself regardless of usefulness (biocentric view). Moral agents are beings that can make moral choices; moral subjects are beings we have duties toward. The Hetch Hetchy case is important because it argued for the inherent value of trees, rocks, and wildlife." }
  ],
